WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.292
TERRY RYAN: This week, we're
talking about a wide range

00:00:02.292 --> 00:00:03.550
of subjects in the cloud.

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:06.220
I'm Terry Ryan, and welcome
to This Week in Cloud,

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.162
where we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:09.162 --> 00:00:10.870
Google Kubernetes
Engine is a great place

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.670
to run your containers,
and running Windows

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:14.050
doesn't change that.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.510
Check out this post for a
step by step guide to running

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:18.670
a Windows container on GKE.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:20.870
And don't worry-- we've
got plenty of code samples

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.252
to give you a running start
in the right direction.

00:00:23.252 --> 00:00:25.210
So copy, paste, and get
your Windows containers

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.920
running on Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:29.920
Moving some or all of
your business to the cloud

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.660
often comes with
a lot of benefits,

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.700
and that's certainly
true for data warehouses.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.820
Still, there are challenges,
from upfront investment

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.950
to handling security risks
to making sure you only

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:40.480
move what you need to move.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.760
This post discusses the
most frequent questions

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.017
that come up from our customers
and help you answer them.

00:00:47.017 --> 00:00:48.850
Cloud functions and
other serverless options

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:51.310
work great when your
whole stack auto scales.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:54.010
But what if your app
depends on APIs or databases

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:56.020
with rate limits or
connection pulls?

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:56.730
Don't worry.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.730
In this post, we'll teach
you all the strategies

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:01.540
for making serverless play nice
with the rest of your stack,

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.400
from tweaking Mac's
instant settings,

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.070
leveraging Cloud Tasks,
using stateful storage,

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:07.775
or plugging in Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:01:07.775 --> 00:01:09.400
We've even got a
decision tree for when

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.310
you need to use each strategy.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:14.350
Fairness in data and
machine learning algorithms

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:17.410
is critical to designing safe
and responsible AI systems

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:18.820
from the ground up.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.380
Learn how to apply
fairness analysis

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.810
at every step of the
model development process.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.150
Then grab the code
samples and get

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.730
started using the What-If
Tool to produce ML models that

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:29.067
are fair for everyone.

00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:31.150
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:33.920
or just see the full
list, check out the link.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.720
Have any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:40.260
Leave us a comment or tweet us,
and we'll see you in the cloud.

